WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
FedRAMP success depends on collaboration among many stakeholders.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.290
Cloud providers, federal agencies, engineers, administrators, security teams,

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:12.000
compliance personnel, assessors, and leadership all contribute to the program.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.015
Cloud service providers are responsible for building and operating cloud offerings,

00:00:16.015 --> 00:00:19.000
implementing controls, maintaining documentation, responding to findings,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.000
and supporting ongoing monitoring activities.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Federal agencies are responsible for understanding their mission needs,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
reviewing available security information, making risk decisions for their own use cases,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.965
and monitoring the services they rely on.

00:00:30.965 --> 00:00:35.235
Assessors evaluate control implementation and provide assessment results.

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:38.875
Security and compliance teams coordinate documentation, evidence, findings,

00:00:38.875 --> 00:00:41.265
risk discussions, and communication across stakeholders.

00:00:41.265 --> 00:00:45.900
Engineers and administrators make FedRAMP real through architecture, identity management,

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:50.000
logging, encryption, vulnerability management, change control, automation,

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.200
incident response, and operational support.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.390
Leadership matters because authorization and risk management require accountable

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:56.205
decisions.

00:00:56.205 --> 00:00:58.950
Leaders provide priorities, resources, expectations,

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.000
and support for remediation and continuous improvement.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.180
The shared responsibility model is important because cloud security is not owned by one

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.000
group alone.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.555
FedRAMP works best when each stakeholder understands what they own and how their work

00:01:10.555 --> 00:01:12.000
affects the security posture.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.965
Clear roles also reduce friction.

00:01:13.965 --> 00:01:18.015
When teams know who owns documentation, who fixes findings, who approves changes,

00:01:18.015 --> 00:01:22.000
and who communicates with assessors or agencies, the program becomes easier to operate.
